1. Cert-alteration / MITM detection by re-probing. Hard constraint: the gateway only sees encrypted traffic. It can read the SNI (hostname) in the ClientHello, but the server certificate is cleartext only under TLS 1.2 — under TLS 1.3, now most traffic, the cert is encrypted. So the gateway can't passively see the cert your client received in order to compare it. Two real options: (a) terminate TLS at the gateway — but that is a MITM, exactly what you're trying to detect, and it needs your CA installed on every client; or (b) run a small client-side agent that reports the SPKI hash of the cert it actually saw, and have the gateway compare. (b) is the honest version of your idea. The re-probe also has a blind spot: if an attacker is on the path your gateway uses too, the gateway sees the same forged cert and the comparison passes — so one vantage point isn't enough. The established techniques for "is this cert legit" without trusting a single path are Certificate Transparency log monitoring and DANE/TLSA where domains publish it. Robust version: client agent reports observed SPKI → gateway compares against its own probe + CT logs + DANE → mismatch flags and you null-route the destination via iptables.
2. Local DNS cache + anti-tampering. Fully real, and the most solid of the four. Run Unbound on the gateway as a validating, caching resolver and force clients to it (the firewall only allows :53 to the box). Unbound does DNSSEC validation natively — that's the actual cryptographic defense against answer tampering, with the caveat that it only covers signed zones, and most domains still aren't signed, so coverage is partial. Close the remaining gap by configuring Unbound's upstream over DoT (forward-tls-upstream) so the path out to the resolver can't be tampered with on the wire either. Local cache + DNSSEC + DoT upstream is the standard, well-supported build, no custom code.
3. File-hash verification, drop unknown, admin-allowlist. The concept (integrity allowlisting) is sound; "add the hash as an iptables rule" is a layer error. iptables matches packets — IPs, ports, protocols — not files. A file doesn't exist until a TCP stream (often a decrypted TLS stream) is reassembled at the application layer, by which point it's already fully arrived; there's no --file-hash match, and the string match only scans bytes inside individual packets, not hashes of reassembled objects. The tool that does what you described is Suricata inline (IPS mode, fed via NFQUEUE): it extracts files from flows, has filesha256/filemd5/filesha1 keywords that match against a hash list, and in inline mode can drop. So "block this file by hash, allow these known-good ones" is native. Your default-deny-unknown model inverts the usual logic and is a strong posture — but be clear-eyed that it breaks essentially every new download until an admin allowlists it, which is heavy outside a tightly scoped network. The TLS wall applies again: Suricata (or the other standard option, Squid + ICAP + ClamAV) can only hash files it can see, so for HTTPS you're back to terminating TLS with your CA on the clients. That's legitimate on a network you administer with the clients' knowledge — it's what corporate web gateways do — but it's an architectural commitment, not a rule.
