The concept works, but three facts about where the VPN server actually sits change how you build it.
MAC addresses never reach the gateway. They're layer-2, stripped at your device's first-hop router; they don't cross the internet, and dev tun is layer-3 with no Ethernet headers at all. So filtering on the client's hardware MAC here isn't possible. The substitute is a cert-bound tunnel IP — pin the iOS device to a fixed tunnel address via OpenVPN's client-config-dir, and that address belongs to whoever holds the client key. An attacker can't take it without the cert, which is a stronger anchor than a MAC anyway.
iOS and macOS are nearly identical at the TCP/IP layer. They share the Darwin stack, so passive TCP fingerprinting (p0f-style) can't reliably separate them — I left it out because it'd just hand you false results. The two signals that do discriminate are the HTTP User-Agent (strong, but cleartext-only unless you terminate TLS) and the TLS JA3/JA4 fingerprint (passive, no decryption, but weaker iOS-vs-macOS separation).
Watch the User-Agent trap: iOS UAs literally contain the string "like Mac OS X". Match on that and you drop every iPhone. The clean discriminator is "Macintosh" — present in desktop macOS UAs, absent from iOS.
On direction: OS fingerprinting is fundamentally an outbound phenomenon. Your device's identity leaks in what it sends, so "if my iOS claims to be a Mac on the way out, drop it" is the natural, enforceable rule. Inbound packets carry the remote server's fingerprint, not your device's — there's no symmetric inbound OS fingerprint to match against. So the inbound piece is honestly a stateful + port heuristic (conntrack already drops inbound you didn't initiate; on top of that I drop inbound to the iOS tunnel IP on desktop-Mac service ports like AFP/ARD), not fingerprinting.

