Right — "verify the syntax before use" was a dodge. Here's the dataset mechanism done properly, and I've rebuilt the JA3 section around it.
The model is two sets and three outcomes:
JA3 in the macOS set → drop + log (Mac impersonation).
JA3 not in the iOS set → log (divergence — which also catches anything that's neither a known iOS nor a known macOS fingerprint).
JA3 in the iOS set → passes silently.
Both sets default empty and behave correctly empty: the macOS set matching nothing means zero drops until you've baselined a Mac and populated it — exactly as you described; the empty iOS set makes the divergence rule log everything, which is your baselining feed, and once you populate it the rule goes quiet except for real divergence.
The detail that actually bites people: ja3.hash is a string buffer, so the dataset type is string, and type string files are base64-encoded on disk, not hex. Hand-writing hex MD5s into the file silently fails to match — nothing ever fires. So the build doesn't hand-write them; it uses a LEARN phase (dataset:set) that has Suricata write entries itself in the correct encoding, which removes the encoding question entirely. You run learn first (drive the iOS device and the Mac through the tunnel), confirm the .lst files fill, then swap to the enforce rules.
Version deps, plainly: datasets need Suricata ≥5 (≥6 for the inline load form to be dependable); JA4 — the stronger, more stable fingerprint — needs ≥7, and on 7+ I'd use ja4.hash with parallel JA4 sets instead. And JA3s drift with OS and app versions, so the LEARN phase isn't one-and-done — re-run it periodically or the divergence log fills with false positives.
I dropped the User-Agent "Macintosh" rule as the mechanism per your call: it's trivially forged and "like Mac OS X" sits in every iOS UA anyway. JA3/JA4 sets carry the detection; UA can only corroborate.

